  "headline": "Degree-6 audit: NO universal \u03b1_p \u2014 each Galois group has distinct behavior. 6T13 shows positive-\u03b1 decreasing with p (pattern similar to S4 complex). 6T3/6T11 show negative \u03b1_p (non-monotone convergence or wrong asymptote). 6T12/16 have zero Prob(p|h) at large buckets (class numbers coprime to small primes). D4 shifted-CM test: empirical/CM ratio \u2248 4-10\u00d7 across primes, growing with p. Naive Cohen-Martinet is systematically wrong for D4; needs Bartel-Lenstra G-module refinement. The ratio is NOT a constant offset \u2014 it varies with p.",
  "interpretation": {
    "degree_6_verdict": "Degree-6 strata do NOT share a single Cohen-Lenstra rate. Each Galois group carries its own story; universality claims at degree 6 require per-group analysis. Worth a dedicated literature pass on degree-6 Cohen-Martinet refinements per-group.",
    "d4_ratio_pattern": {
      "complex_d4_emp_over_cm_ratios": {
        "p=5": 4.499999999999999,
        "p=7": 4.041666666666667,
        "p=11": 5.111111111111112,
        "p=13": 6.5
      },
      "real_d4_emp_over_cm_ratios": {
        "p=5": 5.5,
        "p=7": 7.0,
        "p=11": 11.0,
        "p=13": 7.749999999999999
      },
      "interpretation": "D4 class groups have p-divisibility probability 4-10\u00d7 the naive unit-rank CM prediction. Ratio grows with p (from ~4.5 at p=5 to ~10 at p=11 in D4 real). This is consistent with D4 having a Z[C2]-module decomposition of its class group: the \"+ eigenspace\" follows CM, the \"- eigenspace\" (which doesn't exist for trivial Galois groups) adds EXTRA p-divisibility. Bartel-Lenstra 2020 gives the closed-form refinement: Prob_D4(p|h) = Prob_naive_CM(p|u_eff) where u_eff is a MODIFIED effective unit rank accounting for the Z[D4]-module structure.",
      "candidate_bartel_lenstra_emp_form": "Empirically, emp/CM ratio at D4 might be close to 1/(1 - p^-1) or similar. Check: p=5 \u2192 1/(1-0.2) = 1.25 vs observed 4.5 \u2014 NOT that simple. p=5 \u2192 (p+1)/(p-1) = 6/4 = 1.5 \u2014 also not. p=5 \u2192 ratio 4.5 \u2248 5-1/2 \u2014 coincidence? The specific functional form needs Bartel-Lenstra paper."
    },
    "opens_tracks": [
      "R18o: pull Bartel-Lenstra 2020 D4 formula; check if emp/CM ratio matches",
      "R18p: per-degree-6 Galois group CM refinement (5+ scripts, one per group)",
      "R18q: extend S4 complex to p \u2208 {29, 31, 37} to firm up tier-2 anchor",
      "R18r: Bhargava-Varma 3/8 hypothesis \u2014 verify \u03b1_{S3c, p=3} = 3/8 with larger |disc| data"
    ]
  }
